# No Em Dashes

Do not use em dashes (`—`, Unicode U+2014) or their HTML entity (`&mdash;`) in this codebase. This applies to:

- User-facing copy (labels, titles, subtitles, descriptions, empty states, toasts, banners, tooltips)
- Code comments and JSDoc
- Markdown / documentation inside the repo
- Rule files under `.cursor/rules/`

## What to use instead

- `-` (hyphen-minus, U+002D) - preferred as an inline separator between two related clauses. Surround with spaces when it separates ideas: `Cancel routing - the RFQ stays on the board.`
- `;` (semicolon) - use when the two clauses could each stand alone as sentences and share a close relationship: `The RFQ is submitted; carriers respond within 24 hours.`
- A period + new sentence is always a valid fallback if neither of the above reads naturally.

## Do

```html
<p>Select the metrics the comparison matrix exposes - use the eye icon to preview.</p>
```

```javascript
// Bundle parent row reads like any standard board row - no surface wash.
```

## Do not

```html
<!-- Wrong: em dash character -->
<p>Select the metrics — use the eye icon to preview.</p>

<!-- Wrong: HTML entity form -->
<p>Select the metrics &mdash; use the eye icon to preview.</p>
```

## Enforcement

If a Figma or design reference contains an em dash, still substitute in code. Consistency with this rule wins over 1:1 fidelity to the design copy for punctuation only.

## Notes

- En dash (`–`, U+2013) is also discouraged for the same reason; use `-` for ranges as well (`8 - 12 columns`) unless a numeric range is inside a data cell where a compact glyph is required, in which case prefer `-` too.
- Word "deadline" and similar compounds that contain the letters "dash" are unrelated to this rule.
